{
  "response": "**VERDICT: ACCEPT-WITH-RESERVATIONS**\n\n**Overall Assessment**: The component demonstrates strong foundational accessibility design: native `<button>` elements are used correctly, focus indicators meet WCAG 2.4.13 thresholds, external link behavior is explicitly communicated, and the text alternative strategy aligns with WCAG guidelines for complex content. However, the map placeholder lacks programmatic association to its text alternative, boundary states for zoom controls are unhandled, and third-party map integration leaves assistive technology state communication entirely to chance. These gaps degrade screen reader and keyboard user experience but do not completely block access.\n\n**Pre-commitment Predictions**:\n1. Map placeholder `<div>` will lack `role=\"img\"` or an accessible name, causing AT to skip it or announce it as a generic region.\n2. The text alternative link will be visually present but programmatically unassociated with the map container.\n3. Zoom controls will function but lack disabled states at min/max boundaries, creating dead interactions.\n4. Focus management will work correctly via native tab order; no traps needed.\n5. External link \"opens in new tab\" indicator may duplicate screen reader announcements or be better expressed via `aria-label`.\n\n*Actual findings align with predictions 1\u20133. Predictions 4\u20135 confirmed as acceptable design choices.*\n\n**Critical Findings**:\n*(None strictly critical; no complete access barriers detected.)*\n\n**Major Findings** (significantly degrades experience):\n1. **Map placeholder lacks role and programmatic association to text alternative**\n   - **Evidence**: `src/components/WatershedMapPanel.jsx:14` (`<div className=\"map-canvas\" />`) is an empty container with no ARIA attributes or semantic role. The text alternative link at line 18 is a sibling `<p>` but not programmatically linked.\n   - **User Group**: Screen reader users, cognitive accessibility users\n   - **WCAG/APG Citation**: WCAG 1.3.1 (Info and Relationships), WAI-ARIA Maps Pattern (`role=\"img\"` required for map containers; text alternatives must be programmatically associated)\n   - **Fix**: Add `role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Watershed boundary map\"` to the canvas div. Assign an `id` to the table link container (e.g., `id=\"map-alt-table\"`) and reference it via `aria-describedby=\"map-alt-table\"` on the map div. This explicitly tells AT that the linked table is the map's alternative content.\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n\n2. **Zoom controls lack disabled states at boundary conditions**\n   - **Evidence**: `src/components/WatershedMapPanel.jsx:23-35` buttons always render enabled regardless of map state. `handleZoomIn`/`handleZoomOut` only log to console; no conditional disabling or text updates are implemented.\n   - **User Group**: Keyboard-only users, cognitive accessibility users\n   - **WCAG/APG Citation**: WCAG 4.1.2 (Name, Role, Value), WAI-ARIA APG Composite Widget pattern (interactive controls must reflect current state; disabled buttons should be inert and announce \"disabled\")\n   - **Fix**: Track zoom boundaries in component state. When at max/min zoom, apply `disabled` attribute to the respective button and update visible text (e.g., \"Zoom in\" \u2192 \"Max zoom reached\"). Ensure `aria-disabled=\"true\"` is not needed since native `disabled` handles AT communication.\n   - **Confidence**: HIGH\n\n3. **Third-party map state not exposed to assistive technology**\n   - **Evidence**: `src/components/WatershedMapPanel.jsx:14` mounts a third-party canvas. The component does not expose zoom/pan state updates via `aria-live`, `aria-busy`, or button state sync.\n   - **User Group**: Screen reader users\n   - **WCAG/APG Citation**: WCAG 4.1.3 (Status Messages), WAI-ARIA Maps Pattern (map interactions should announce state changes if the library doesn't handle it internally)\n   - **Fix**: If the third-party library does not manage its own ARIA, add a visually hidden `<div aria-live=\"polite\" role=\"status\">` below the controls. Update its text content on zoom/reset to reflect current state (e.g., \"Map zoomed in to 150%\"). Ensure updates are debounced to avoid announcement flooding.\n   - **Confidence**: MEDIUM\n\n**Minor Findings** (friction but workaround exists):\n- External link hidden text duplication: `src/components/WatershedMapPanel.jsx:28` uses a visually hidden span for \"(opens in a new tab)\". While acceptable, some ATs read it aloud redundantly. Consider moving the full accessible name to the `<a>` via `aria-label=\"Open full watershed report (opens in a new tab)\"` and removing the span for cleaner DOM.\n- Focus indicator contrast: `src/components/WatershedMapPanel.css:34-36` uses `#0b5fff` outline on white background (~4.53:1). Passes WCAG 2.4.13, but ensure it remains visible when the button is in a disabled or low-opacity state.\n\n**Enhancements** (best practice not met):\n- Add `aria-current=\"page\"` to the map heading if this panel represents the active view in a multi-panel interface.\n- Consider adding `tabIndex={0}` and `role=\"toolbar\"` to `.map-controls` if these buttons function as a cohesive control group, enabling arrow key navigation within the group per WAI-ARIA Toolbar pattern.\n\n**What's Missing** (gaps, unhandled edge cases, unstated assumptions):\n- Programmatic association between map container and text alternative table link\n- Boundary state handling for zoom controls (disabled at limits)\n- Live region or state sync for third-party map interactions\n- Explicit `aria-label` on the map canvas to prevent AT from announcing it as an empty region\n\n**Multi-Perspective Notes**:\n| Perspective | Alarm Level | Trigger Signal |\n|-------------|-------------|----------------|\n| Screen reader | HIGH | Map placeholder lacks role/label; text alternative unlinked programmatically |\n| Keyboard-only | MEDIUM | Buttons work but lack disabled states at boundaries; no toolbar roving tabindex |\n| Low vision | LOW | Focus indicators meet thresholds; layout reflows cleanly at 200% zoom |\n| Cognitive | MEDIUM | Dead button interactions at zoom limits may confuse users; external link behavior is clear |\n| Vestibular & motion | LOW | No animations or auto-playing content in component |\n| Auditory access | LOW | No audio/video elements present |\n| Environmental contrast | LOW | Button text/background passes 4.5:1; focus outline meets 3:1 perimeter requirement |\n\n**Verdict Justification**: The component's core interaction model is sound and uses native semantics correctly. The reservations stem from the map placeholder being invisible to assistive technology without programmatic association, and zoom controls lacking boundary state feedback. These are design completeness gaps rather than compliance violations. Upgrading to ACCEPT requires: (1) linking the map div to its text alternative via `aria-describedby`, (2) implementing disabled states at zoom boundaries, and (3) ensuring third-party map updates are communicated to AT or handled internally by the library.\n\n**Open Questions (unscored)**:\n- Does the third-party map library automatically manage ARIA state for zoom/pan interactions? If yes, Phase 5 finding #3 can be downgraded.\n- Is this panel part of a larger dashboard where `role=\"toolbar\"` on `.map-controls` would improve keyboard navigation coherence?\n- Should the text alternative table link be placed inside an `<address>` or `<aside>` to better signal its supplementary nature per WCAG 1.4.10 Reflow guidelines?\n\n**Phase Execution Summary**:\n- Phase 0: No test evidence provided; proceeded with design review. Measurement of third-party map ARIA behavior would strengthen findings.\n- Phase 1: Predictions matched actual code structure and interaction model.\n- Phase 2: Semantic HTML audit confirmed native `<button>`, correct heading hierarchy, proper landmark usage (`<section>`), and valid external link pattern.\n- Phase 3: ARIA pattern audit identified missing `role=\"img\"` on map container and unlinked text alternative per WAI-ARIA Maps pattern.\n- Phase 4: Focus management reviewed; tab order is logical, focus indicators meet WCAG 2.4.13, no traps present.\n- Phase 5: State communication audit flagged missing boundary states and third-party map state exposure.\n- Phase 6: Multi-perspective review completed; screen reader and keyboard perspectives flagged as MEDIUM/HIGH alarm.\n- Phase 7: Gap analysis identified programmatic association, disabled states, and live region gaps.\n- Phase 8: Realist Check applied; severity calibrated to MAJOR for design completeness gaps (not blocking but degrades experience).\n- Phase 9: Self-audit completed; all findings rated HIGH/MEDIUM confidence with clear evidence. No preferences mislabeled as gaps.\n- Phase 10: Synthesis confirms ACCEPT-WITH-RESERVATIONS pending programmatic association and boundary state fixes.",
